<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guanqiu Jian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 255</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guanqiu Jian was a general of the Chinese state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a>. In 244-245 he invaded Goguryeo, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dongcheon-of-goguryeo">King Dongcheon</a> and sacked the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hwando">Hwando</a>, pursuing the king into the Okjeo country. In 255 he rebelled against the Sima family, who controlled the Wei court, and was defeated and killed.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Guanqiu Jian, whose courtesy name was Zhonggong, came from Wenxi County in Hedong Commandery, present-day Wenxi County in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>. His father, Guanqiu Xing, served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> as Administrator of Anding Commandery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-pi">Cao Pi</a> as Administrator of Wuwei Commandery, and held the peerage Marquis of Gaoyang District. Guanqiu Jian inherited the peerage and served as a clerk to the Marquis of Pingyuan, and he had been an assistant official to Cao Rui while the future emperor was crown prince.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Cao Rui became the second emperor of Wei in 226, he made his former official a Gentleman of Writing and supervisor of the Imperial Guards. As Agricultural Officer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, Guanqiu Jian urged the emperor to scale down his palace building. He was later Inspector of Jing Province, and in the Qinglong era (233-237), when Cao Rui planned to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>, Inspector of You Province and Colonel Who Protects the Wuhuan.</p><p>In 237 he marched against the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongsun-yuan">Gongsun Yuan</a>, a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> who had rebelled, but heavy flooding ended the campaign. The next year he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-yi">Sima Yi</a> defeated Gongsun Yuan, and he was raised to county marquis as Marquis of Anyi. In the war against Goguryeo he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dongcheon-of-goguryeo">King Dongcheon</a> near the Tongjia River; parts of his army reached the eastern coast of the peninsula and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> before withdrawing. A fragment of the stele carved for the victory was found in 1905.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The regent Sima Shi, in power since the death of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-yi">Sima Yi</a> in 251, purged Xiahou Xuan and Li Feng in 254. Chen Shou records in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms">Sanguozhi</a> that Guanqiu Jian, close to both, rebelled out of loyalty to the emperor Cao Mao and for fear of sharing their fate. He rose at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shouchun">Shouchun</a> with Wen Qin, but the revolt was crushed within months; fleeing to Shen County, he was killed by one Zhang Shu. Most of his family were killed, and the survivors defected to Eastern Wu.</p><p>His surname is often read as Wuqiu, as in Sima Guang's Zizhi Tongjian. The scholar Wu Jinhua argued that Guanqiu was a copyist's error of the Tang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> dynasties, and that Wuqiu and Manqiu were readings of an original Muqiu.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guanqiu Jian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Setbacks came early: in 244 the Cao Wei general <b>Guanqiu Jian</b> sacked the capital Hwando, and in 342 the Former Yan of the Murong Xianbei sacked it again, carrying off the queen mother and the body of the previous king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wenxi County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Feidong County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.35,111.22&amp;year=245" aria-label="Wenxi County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7363 886 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Guanqiu Jian"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7813 1156h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7813 1156h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.35,111.22&amp;year=245">What was here in 245 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
